<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/5th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Greco-Persian Wars</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">499–449 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Also known as Greek Wars, Persian Wars · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Greco-Persian Wars were a series of conflicts in the fifth century BCE between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-city-states">Greek city-states</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>. Persian invasions of mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> were repulsed at battles such as Marathon, Salamis and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a>. For the Achaemenid Empire the wars marked the western limit of its expansion and shaped later Greek views of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/greco-persian-wars.jpg" alt="Greco-Persian Wars" width="320" height="317" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greek-Persian_duel_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The conflict began when the Persian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> conquered the Greek-inhabited region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a> in 547 BCE. In 499 BCE the tyrant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristagoras-of-miletus">Aristagoras of Miletus</a> mounted a failed expedition against Naxos with Persian support and, sensing his dismissal, incited Ionia to revolt against King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius the Great</a>. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and Eretria he captured and razed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a> in 498 BCE, and Darius vowed revenge. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> won a decisive victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lade">Battle of Lade</a> in 494 BCE, and the revolt collapsed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In 492 BCE Mardonius re-subjugated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> a fully subordinate client kingdom, but a storm wrecked his fleet off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos">Mount Athos</a> and he withdrew. In 490 BCE Datis and Artaphernes, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a>'s son, crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>, took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyclades">Cyclades</a> and razed Eretria, but the Athenians and Plataeans defeated them at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-marathon">Battle of Marathon</a>. Darius died in 486 BCE, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a> crushed a revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and prepared a full-scale invasion.</p><p>In 480 BCE Xerxes led one of the largest armies of the ancient world into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Leonidas I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> held the pass of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae">Thermopylae</a> with 300 of his bodyguard and allied contingents, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> won, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a>, and razed an evacuated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. The combined Greek fleet then defeated the Persian fleet at Salamis in September. In 479 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> routed the Persian army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Battle of Plataea</a>, and the victory at Mycale prompted a second revolt of the Greek cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> then took the offensive. They besieged and took Sestos and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, which gave them command of the straits and access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> trade. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delian-league">Delian League</a>, which the Athenians came to lead, and the Athenian politician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimon">Cimon</a>, the Greeks cleared Persian garrisons from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> in the 470s, and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-eurymedon">Battle of the Eurymedon</a> the Athenians destroyed a Persian fleet and routed its army ashore.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> mentions no peace after the Battle of Salamis-in-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, while Diodorus reports a Peace of Callias, which some fourth-century authors rejected. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> did not release its allies from their duty to provide money or ships, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delian-league">Delian League</a> became the Athenian Empire. The rivalry between Athens and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> later led to the Second Peloponnesian War.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Greco-Persian Wars” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Greek Wars</b> showed the limits of Persian arms against determined Greek hoplites and fleets, at Marathon (490 BCE), Salamis (480 BCE) and Plataea (479 BCE).</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Greco-Persian Wars</b> began with the conquest of the Greek cities of Ionia by the Achaemenid Persian Empire of Cyrus the Great shortly after 550 BC.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delian-league">Delian League · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Miletus was the leading city of archaic Ionia: a trading power that planted dozens of colonies around the Black Sea, the home of Thales, Anaximander and Anaximenes, and the instigator of the Ionian Revolt, whose destruction by Persia in 494 BCE helped set off the <b>Persian Wars</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/miletus">Miletus · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Thebes was also a member of the Delphic amphictyony, from which Sparta tried and failed to expel it after the <b>Persian Wars</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/thebes">Thebes · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Parthenon was built in the 5th century BCE in thanksgiving for the Greek victory over the Persian invaders in the <b>Greco-Persian Wars</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/parthenon">Parthenon · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
